<h1>Incident Response</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">When Gate finds a secret in pushed history, a full incident response workflow begins: rotate, audit, clean, scrub, and document.</p>

    <h2 id="when-ir-triggers">When Incident Response Triggers</h2>

    <p>Incident response is triggered when Gate determines that a credential has been pushed to a remote repository. This is the highest-severity exposure state -- the secret may have been visible to anyone with repository access, and may have been indexed by third-party tools or scrapers.</p>

    <div class="warn-box">
      <strong>Exposure level: PUSHED</strong> -- a secret was found in remote-tracking branches (e.g., <code>origin/main</code>). Full incident response is required. Assume the credential is compromised.
    </div>

    <p>Gate detects the PUSHED state by inspecting <code>git log --remotes</code> to check whether the commit containing the finding exists in any remote-tracking ref. If it does, exposure is classified as PUSHED regardless of whether the branch was subsequently force-pushed or deleted.</p>

    <h2 id="exposure-assessment">Exposure Assessment</h2>

    <p>Before triggering the response workflow, Gate determines the exposure level of each finding. This assessment drives which steps are required.</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Level</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
          <th>Required action</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>LOCAL</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-2);">Staged but never committed. File is dirty or in the index only.</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-3);">Extract the secret. No history cleanup needed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>COMMITTED</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-2);">In local commits, but not yet pushed to any remote.</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-3);">Extract the secret, then amend or rebase to remove it from local history.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PUSHED</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-2);">In at least one remote-tracking branch.</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-3);">Full incident response: rotate, audit, clean, scrub history, document.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>UNKNOWN</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-2);">Gate cannot determine git state (not a git repo, detached HEAD, etc.).</td>
          <td style="color: var(--text-3);">Treat as PUSHED. Rotate immediately.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Gate determines exposure by running <code>git log --all --find-object</code> and cross-referencing the containing commit against remote-tracking refs via <code>git branch -r --contains &lt;commit&gt;</code>. If the git working directory is unavailable, exposure defaults to UNKNOWN.</p>

    <h2 id="workflow">The 5-Step Response Workflow</h2>

    <p>When exposure is PUSHED (or UNKNOWN), Gate walks through five steps in order. Each step is tracked and recorded in the incident document.</p>

    <h3>Step 1: ROTATE</h3>

    <p>Rotation is always the first step. A pushed secret must be considered compromised immediately -- even if no unauthorized access has occurred yet. Gate provides provider-specific rotation guidance based on the rule that matched.</p>

    <p>For credentials from supported providers, Gate detects the relevant CLI tool and prints exact commands:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">  [ROTATE]  AWS IAM Access Key detected</span>

<span class="dim">  CLI detected: aws</span>

<span class="prompt">  $ </span><span class="cmd">aws iam delete-access-key --access-key-id AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE</span>
<span class="prompt">  $ </span><span class="cmd">aws iam create-access-key --user-name &lt;your-user&gt;</span>

<span class="dim">  Or rotate in the console:</span>
<span class="dim">  https://console.aws.amazon.com/iam/home#/security_credentials</span></div>

    <p>Provider rotation reference:</p>

