    <h3>Step 2: AUDIT</h3>

    <p>After rotating, check provider access logs for any usage of the exposed credential during the exposure window. Gate prints the exposure window (from the earliest commit timestamp to now) and provides direct links to each provider's audit log interface.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">  [AUDIT]  Exposure window: 2025-11-14T09:22:00Z &rarr; now</span>

<span class="dim">  AWS CloudTrail:</span>
<span class="dim">  https://console.aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/home#/events</span>
<span class="dim">  Filter: AccessKeyId = AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE, time range: exposure window</span>

<span class="dim">  GitHub audit log:</span>
<span class="dim">  https://github.com/settings/security-log</span>
<span class="dim">  Filter: actor actions during the exposure window</span></div>

    <p>Look for any API calls, logins, or resource access that did not originate from your own systems. Pay particular attention to calls from unfamiliar IPs or user agents, and to actions you did not initiate (resource creation, data exports, permission changes).</p>

    <h3>Step 3: CLEAN</h3>

    <p>The CLEAN step removes the secret from your working code. Gate calls the auto-fix engine to extract the credential from the source file and rewrite the reference to use an environment variable.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">  [CLEAN]  Running auto-fix on src/api/payments.ts:23</span>

<span class="ok">  Extracted: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY &rarr; .env</span>
<span class="ok">  Rewrote:   src/api/payments.ts (process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY)</span>
<span class="dim">  .env permissions: 0o600</span></div>

    <p>This step only cleans the current working tree. The secret still exists in git history, which is addressed in the next step. See <a href="/gate/wiki/auto-fix/">Auto-Fix Engine</a> for details on how fixers work.</p>

    <h3>Step 4: SCRUB</h3>

    <p>The SCRUB step generates a script to purge the secret from the entire git history. Gate creates a <code>git-filter-repo</code> replacements file and a shell script to run it.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">  [SCRUB]  Generating history purge script</span>

<span class="ok">  ~/.gate/purge-abc123.sh       (permissions: 0o700)</span>
<span class="ok">  ~/.gate/replacements-abc123   (permissions: 0o600)</span>

<span class="dim">  Contents of purge-abc123.sh:</span>
<span class="cmt">  #!/bin/sh
  # Gate incident purge script -- generated 2026-01-15T14:32:00Z
  # Review before running. This rewrites git history.
  #
  # Prerequisites: pip install git-filter-repo
  git filter-repo \
    --replace-text ~/.gate/replacements-abc123 \
    --force
  #
  # After verifying the rewrite, force-push all branches:
  # git push --force-with-lease --all
  # git push --force-with-lease --tags
  #
  # BFG alternative (if git-filter-repo is unavailable):
  # bfg --replace-text ~/.gate/replacements-abc123</span></div>

    <p>The replacements file contains the literal secret value mapped to <code>***REMOVED***</code>. The force-push commands are included as comments -- you must uncomment and run them manually after verifying the rewritten history. Coordinate with all repository collaborators before force-pushing, as their local clones will need to be rebased or re-cloned.</p>

    <h3>Step 5: DOCUMENT</h3>

    <p>Gate creates a permanent incident record and generates a formal Markdown report.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">  [DOCUMENT]  Creating incident record</span>

<span class="ok">  ~/.gate/incidents/inc-20260115-abc123.json</span>
<span class="ok">  ~/.gate/reports/incident-abc123.md</span></div>

    <h2 id="incident-records">Incident Record Format</h2>

    <p>Each incident is stored as a JSON file in <code>~/.gate/incidents/</code> with permissions 0o600. The record captures the full context of the finding, every action taken, and references to applicable compliance frameworks.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">{
  "id":            "inc-20260115-abc123",
  "detectedAt":   "2026-01-15T14:32:00Z",
  "ruleId":        "stripe-secret-key",
  "severity":      "CRITICAL",
  "file":          "src/api/payments.ts",
  "line":          23,
  "exposure":      "PUSHED",
  "exposureWindow": {
    "from": "2025-11-14T09:22:00Z",
    "to":   "2026-01-15T14:32:00Z"
  },
  "actions": [
    { "step": "ROTATE",   "completedAt": "2026-01-15T14:35:00Z", "by": "user" },
    { "step": "AUDIT",    "completedAt": "2026-01-15T14:40:00Z", "by": "user" },
    { "step": "CLEAN",    "completedAt": "2026-01-15T14:40:01Z", "by": "gate" },
    { "step": "SCRUB",    "completedAt": "2026-01-15T14:41:00Z", "by": "gate" },
    { "step": "DOCUMENT", "completedAt": "2026-01-15T14:41:02Z", "by": "gate" }
  ],
  "compliance": [
    "OWASP-A02", "NIST-800-63B", "CIS-16.7", "SOC2-CC6.6"
  ],
  "purgeScript": "~/.gate/purge-abc123.sh",
  "report":      "~/.gate/reports/incident-abc123.md"
}</span></div>

    <h2 id="incident-reports">Incident Reports</h2>

    <p>Gate can generate a formal Markdown incident report from any stored incident record. This report is suitable for sharing with security teams, auditors, or management.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --incident inc-20260115-abc123</span></div>

    <p>The generated report includes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Executive summary</strong> -- finding, severity, exposure level, and key dates</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Timeline</strong> -- when the secret was introduced, when it was detected, and when each remediation step was completed</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Actions taken</strong> -- which steps were completed, by whom, and at what time</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Compliance references</strong> -- which controls this incident relates to (OWASP, NIST, CIS, SOC2)</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Recommendations</strong> -- provider-specific guidance to prevent recurrence</li>
    </ul>

    <p>To list all stored incidents:</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --list-incidents</span></div>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Tip:</strong> Incident records and reports are stored locally in <code>~/.gate/incidents/</code> and <code>~/.gate/reports/</code> with owner-only permissions (0o600). They are never sent to any remote service.
    </div>
